 482: \section{Integrity Checks and Packet--Flow Alignment}
 483: \label{subsec:data_integrity}
 484: 
 485: Preprocessing begins by normalising column names and discarding \datasetfield{record_type}, a logging descriptor with no predictive role. Infinite values are converted to missing entries. Rows are rejected when \datasetfield{flow_id} is zero or missing; valid flow rows also require \datasetfield{flow_start_timestamp_us}, while packet rows require \datasetfield{timestamp_us}, in each case with a nonzero, nonmissing value.
 486: 
 487: Only one effective flow row is retained for each \datasetfield{flow_id}. When duplicates occur, a stable sort by identifier and start time is applied, the first record is kept, and the number of discarded duplicates is logged. The identifier sets of the packet and flow tables are then intersected so that unmatched rows can be removed from both views. A final sort places packets in \datasetfield{flow_id} and \datasetfield{timestamp_us} order. Together, these operations establish the one-to-many relation
 488: \begin{equation}
 489:     f \longleftrightarrow
 490:     \left\{p_{f,1},p_{f,2},\ldots,p_{f,n_f}\right\},
 491:     \qquad
 492:     t_{f,1}\leq t_{f,2}\leq\cdots\leq t_{f,n_f},
 493:     \label{eq:packet_flow_alignment}
 494: \end{equation}
 495: before splitting or feature transformation. Table~\ref{tab:raw_dataset_summary} describes the CSV files as extracted, whereas post-cleaning counts for a particular run are determined from its saved split report.
 496: 
 497: \section{Feature Selection and Leakage Control}
 498: \label{subsec:feature_selection}
 499: 
 500: Raw columns are not passed wholesale to the network; the input schema is controlled by configuration. Before one-hot expansion, the principal Stage~1 setup uses the 23 packet predictors listed in Table~\ref{tab:stage1_packet_features}. Numerical variables encode packet and header dimensions, categorical variables describe protocol state, and binary indicators retain direction, transport presence, and individual TCP flags.
 501: 
 502: \begin{table}[H]
 503:     \centering
 504:     \caption{Packet predictors used by the principal Stage~1 configuration.}
 505:     \label{tab:stage1_packet_features}
 506:     \small
 507:     \begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.5cm}p{11.4cm}@{}}
 508:         \toprule
 509:         Type & Selected raw columns \\
 510:         \midrule
 511:         Numerical (7) &
 512:         \datasetfield{pkt_len}, \datasetfield{ip_len},
 513:         \datasetfield{payload_len}, \datasetfield{ttl_or_hop_limit},
 514:         \datasetfield{tcp_window}, \datasetfield{tcp_header_len},
 515:         \datasetfield{udp_len} \\
 516:         Categorical (4) &
 517:         \datasetfield{ip_version}, \datasetfield{protocol},
 518:         \datasetfield{icmp_type}, \datasetfield{icmp_code} \\
 519:         Binary (12) &
 520:         \datasetfield{direction}, \datasetfield{has_tcp},
 521:         \datasetfield{has_udp}, \datasetfield{has_icmp},
 522:         \datasetfield{tcp_fin}, \datasetfield{tcp_syn},
 523:         \datasetfield{tcp_rst}, \datasetfield{tcp_psh},
 524:         \datasetfield{tcp_ack_flag}, \datasetfield{tcp_urg},
 525:         \datasetfield{tcp_ece}, \datasetfield{tcp_cwr} \\
 526:         \bottomrule
 527:     \end{tabular}
 528: \end{table}
 529: 
 530: Stage~1 excludes raw IP addresses and ports, packet and flow identifiers, absolute timestamps, TCP sequence and acknowledgement numbers, the combined TCP-flag integer, \datasetfield{l7_proto}, and both targets. Their removal discourages memorisation of site-specific endpoints and prevents identifiers or capture position from becoming target shortcuts. Relative timing is retained separately through a tensor derived from \datasetfield{flow_iat_us}; the absolute packet timestamp is not supplied as an ordinary feature.
 531: 
 532: When statistical fusion is enabled, a configured subset of Table~\ref{tab:flow_schema} is processed through a separate branch. The principal subset covers duration, directional packet and byte totals, length distributions, rates, inter-arrival summaries, flag counts, header lengths, segment and window measurements, and selected active-period statistics. Availability of the two initial-window measurements is represented by binary indicators. Endpoint identities and ports, absolute flow times, and \datasetfield{label} are omitted. Since the packet-only ablation disables this branch, presence in the raw schema does not imply use in every experiment.
 533: 
 534: Three metadata fields accompany every Stage~1 embedding: \datasetfield{flow_start_timestamp_us}, \datasetfield{source_ip}, and \datasetfield{destination_ip}. They define relations for Stage~2 and are not Stage~1 predictors. Host equality determines source-host, destination-host, and endpoint contexts, while start time establishes ordering. Addresses are never one-hot encoded as semantic features, and historical labels are not retrieved into context. The required relations are thereby preserved without directly training on host identity.
 535: 
 536: \section{Train-Fitted Feature Transformations}
 537: \label{subsec:feature_transformations}
 538: 
 539: Every data-dependent transformation is estimated from the training partition and subsequently applied unchanged to validation and test records. Estimating clipping limits, scaling statistics, or category vocabularies from the complete dataset would expose information from later partitions \cite{Arp2022DosDonts}. Packet and flow variables follow pipelines of the same form, but their fitted parameters remain separate.
 540: 
 541: \paragraph{Numerical features.}
 542: After conversion to numeric form, non-finite entries are marked as missing. Selected nonnegative variables with heavy-tailed distributions undergo the transform
 543: \begin{equation}
 544:     \widetilde{x}=\log(1+\max(x,0)).
 545:     \label{eq:log1p_preprocessing}
 546: \end{equation}
 547: Configured variables include packet and payload lengths, TCP window, header length, UDP length, and numerous flow-level counts, durations, byte totals, rates, and inter-arrival summaries. Zero imputation is applied after the logarithmic transform.
 548: 
 549: For numerical variable $j$, lower and upper clipping limits are the training quantiles $q_{0.001,j}^{\mathrm{tr}}$ and $q_{0.999,j}^{\mathrm{tr}}$. Winsorisation in every partition is defined by
 550: \begin{equation}
 551:     x_{ij}^{\mathrm{clip}}
 552:     = \min\!\left(
 553:         \max\!\left(\widetilde{x}_{ij},q_{0.001,j}^{\mathrm{tr}}\right),
 554:         q_{0.999,j}^{\mathrm{tr}}
 555:       \right).
 556:     \label{eq:quantile_clipping}
 557: \end{equation}
 558: Robust scaling is then applied in the principal configuration:
 559: \begin{equation}
 560:     x_{ij}^{\mathrm{scaled}}
 561:     = \frac{x_{ij}^{\mathrm{clip}}-
 562:              \operatorname{median}_{\mathrm{tr}}(x_j)}
 563:             {\operatorname{IQR}_{\mathrm{tr}}(x_j)},
 564:     \label{eq:robust_scaling}
 565: \end{equation}
 566: where the denominator is computed from the training interquartile range. Logarithmic compression, quantile clipping, and robust scaling limit the influence of extreme bursts without drawing statistics from the test partition.
 567: 
 568: \paragraph{Categorical features.}
 569: An explicit \datasetfield{MISSING} token represents absent categorical values. The one-hot vocabulary is learned from training categories; categories encountered only during validation or testing are ignored, leaving the input dimension unchanged.
 570: 
 571: \paragraph{Binary features.}
 572: Binary fields are first converted to numbers. Missing and non-finite entries become zero, as do all non-positive values, while positive entries become one. No one-hot encoding, logarithmic transform, clipping, or scaling is applied to these indicators.
 573: 
 574: \section{Packet-Sequence Construction}
 575: \label{subsec:sequence_construction}
 576: 
 577: Packets in aligned flow $f$ are sorted by \datasetfield{timestamp_us}. Under the principal \datasetfield{head} policy, at most $L=128$ packets are retained. Flows longer than $L$ contribute their first 128 observations; shorter sequences are padded with zeros on the right. Valid positions are recorded in a Boolean mask so that padding is excluded from attention and pooling. The resulting tensor shapes are
 578: \begin{equation}
 579:     \mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times L\times d_p},\qquad
 580:     \mathbf{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times L},\qquad
 581:     \mathbf{M}\in\{0,1\}^{N\times L},
 582:     \label{eq:stage1_tensor_shapes}
 583: \end{equation}
 584: with $d_p$ denoting the transformed packet-feature dimension after one-hot encoding.
 585: 
 586: Nonnegative within-flow inter-arrival times define the timing tensor:
 587: \begin{equation}
 588:     T_{f,i}=\log\!\left(1+\max(\Delta t_{f,i},0)\right),
 589:     \qquad
 590:     \Delta t_{f,i}=\datasetfield{flow_iat_us}(p_{f,i}).
 591:     \label{eq:time_tensor}
 592: \end{equation}
 593: This tensor enters the time-aware encoder separately; absolute timestamps are not concatenated to the packet features.
 594: 
 595: Two additional policies support the truncation ablation. \datasetfield{head_tail} combines the first $\lfloor L/2\rfloor$ packets with the final $L-\lfloor L/2\rfloor$ packets. \datasetfield{random} samples $L$ positions without replacement from a deterministic seed derived from \datasetfield{flow_id} and the global seed, after which the selected packets are returned to chronological order. Separate experiments consider $L\in\{8,16,32,64,128,256\}$. Only the retained evidence and associated computation change; flow membership in each split remains fixed.
 596: 
 597: \section{Chronological Train--Validation--Test Partition}
 598: \label{subsec:chronological_partition}
 599: 
 600: Unique flows are stably ordered by \datasetfield{flow_start_timestamp_us} before a flow-level split is made. The earliest 70\% form the training partition, the following 10\% form validation, and the final 20\% are reserved for testing. With \datasetfield{stratify=false}, these chronological boundaries are not adjusted to equalise class proportions. For the $421{,}898$ aligned records, this procedure yields the partition sizes in Table~\ref{tab:chronological_split}.
 601: 
 602: \begin{table}[H]
 603:     \centering
 604:     \caption{Principal chronological flow partition.}
 605:     \label{tab:chronological_split}
 606:     \begin{tabular}{@{}lrrl@{}}
 607:         \toprule
 608:         Partition & Ratio & Number of flows & Temporal role \\
 609:         \midrule
 610:         Training & 70\% & 295,329 & Earliest flows \\
 611:         Validation & 10\% & 42,189 & Intermediate flows \\
 612:         Test & 20\% & 84,380 & Latest flows \\
 613:         \midrule
 614:         Total & 100\% & 421,898 & \\
 615:         \bottomrule
 616:     \end{tabular}
 617: \end{table}
 618: 
 619: Every packet follows the partition assignment of its parent \datasetfield{flow_id}; no flow can span two partitions. Only training-flow packets and statistics are used to fit preprocessing. Validation data support hyperparameter choice, early stopping, and decision-threshold selection, while final evaluation is confined to the test partition. Relative to random row splitting, chronological separation more closely reflects deployment on later traffic and reduces leakage from distributing related packets or temporally adjacent behaviour across partitions \cite{SommerPaxson2010,Arp2022DosDonts}.
 620: 
 621: \section{Stage~1 Artifacts Supplied to Stage~2}
 622: \label{subsec:stage2_artifacts}
 623: 
 624: Once Stage~1 has been trained, exactly one embedding and one metadata record are exported for each flow in every partition. Metadata comprise \datasetfield{flow_id}, the target, partition name, \datasetfield{flow_start_timestamp_us}, and source and destination identifiers. The embedding provides Stage~2 content; time and host identity are used only to determine which earlier flows are eligible. Retaining the partition name guards against unintended context construction across split boundaries. Targets remain available for training and evaluation but never become context features.
 625: 
 626: Each Stage~1 run also records the selected features, encoder categories, clipping limits, scaling parameters, sequence policy, random seed, ordered flow identifiers, partition-level class counts, model configuration, and checkpoint identity. These records link every Stage~2 result to the Stage~1 checkpoint and split manifest from which its embeddings originated.
 627: 
 628: \section{Validity, Privacy, and Reproducibility Considerations}
 629: \label{subsec:dataset_limitations}
 630: 
 631: Although the resulting dataset supports evaluation on a large, temporally ordered enterprise trace, four constraints delimit the conclusions.
 632: 
 633: \begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{L\arabic*:},leftmargin=*]
 634:     \item \textbf{Alert-derived ground truth.} Label coverage and error reflect a single Suricata ruleset. A negative record is therefore more accurately interpreted as ``no alert observed'' than as independently verified benign traffic.
 635: 
 636:     \item \textbf{Single-trace scope.} Evidence from one company capture cannot establish generalisation across organisations, sensors, or extended periods. Evaluation on external PCAPs remains necessary.
 637: 
 638:     \item \textbf{Capture visibility.} Only traffic observable at the collection point appears in the PCAP. Packet loss, asymmetric routing, encryption, and incomplete flows may change the extracted measurements. Where organisational policy allows, documentation should include the collection tool, interface location, capture interval, packet-loss report, and snap length.
 639: 
 640:     \item \textbf{Sensitive identifiers.} Stage~2 relations are currently formed from addresses retained in local metadata. Those addresses must be excluded from the thesis, shared CSVs, screenshots, and public metadata. Any released artefact requires stable pseudonymisation verified to preserve equality-based context membership.
 641: \end{enumerate}
 642: 
 643: Within these limits, the study evaluates a traceable learning pipeline on a large proprietary capture with weak labels. Suricata outputs are not treated as perfect attack truth, the presence of every zero-day behaviour is not assumed, and no claim is made that performance transfers unchanged to other enterprise networks. Tensors, masks, flow measurements, and causal-context metadata together define the interface passed between the two model stages.
 644: 
