1650: \section{Conclusions}
1651: \label{subsec:conclusion_rq_answers}
1652: 
1653: \paragraph{RQ1: intra-flow representation.}
1654: RQ1 is answered in favour of modelling packets as an ordered, time-aware sequence rather than relying only on aggregate flow statistics. Ordinal position and elapsed time provide distinct and complementary information about how a flow develops, while a separate statistical conditioning and fusion pathway further improves the complete Stage~1 representation. The sequence-length experiments qualify this conclusion by showing that observing more packets does not necessarily lead to better performance.
1655: 
1656: \paragraph{RQ2: cross-flow context.}
1657: RQ2 is answered by showing that selective historical context can improve classification beyond independent flow prediction. Among the tested relations, source-host context provides the strongest overall balance, whereas endpoint context shifts the operating point towards higher recall at the cost of more false positives. Across the evaluated Stage~2 architectures, the proposed target-query model achieves the strongest overall performance.
1658: 
1659: Together, these findings answer the central research question by showing that intra-flow temporal structure and selective inter-flow context provide complementary information at different levels of the detection hierarchy. Temporal structure describes how a flow develops internally, while selective historical context relates that representation to earlier network activity. Combining these two levels improves flow-level intrusion detection on the evaluated capture.The main contribution of this thesis is therefore not the use of a Transformer architecture in isolation, but the design and evaluation of a two-stage hierarchical decomposition that explicitly combines these complementary sources of evidence.
