1271:     \item \textbf{No-context MLP} classifies only the current Stage~1 embedding and provides a minimal current-flow-only lower bound. Because it is not capacity matched to the contextual encoders, it is not by itself a causal estimate of the value of history.
1272: 
1273:     \item \textbf{Vanilla Transformer} applies full self-attention to the same context sequence and classifies its last valid token \cite{Vaswani2017}.
1274: 
1275:     \item \textbf{LSTM} and \textbf{GRU} replace attention with recurrent context encoders while preserving the Stage~1 inputs, context window, masks, and classifier protocol \cite{Hochreiter1997LSTM,Cho2014GRU}.
1276: 
1277:     \item \textbf{CNN+LSTM} applies parallel temporal convolutions with kernel sizes 3, 5, and 7 before a two-layer LSTM, combining local pattern extraction with recurrent context modelling \cite{Kim2014CNN,Hochreiter1997LSTM}.
1278: \end{itemize}
1279: 
1280: During architecture comparison, each contextual baseline receives the same \texttt{source\_host} histories at $W=128$, with the current flow occupying the final valid position. Since the target consumes one position, at most 127 historical flows are available. The no-context control keeps the target embedding and discards the preceding history.
1281: 
1282: Relation sensitivity holds the proposed encoder and $W=128$ fixed while varying eligibility among \texttt{time\_only}, \texttt{source\_host}, \texttt{destination\_host}, and \texttt{endpoint}. Window sensitivity instead fixes \texttt{source\_host} and evaluates $W\in\{16,32,64,128,256\}$. Run summaries contain predictive metrics and confusion matrices but do not retain target-level context occupancy or age. Interpretation of the window sweep therefore acknowledges that missing diagnostic information.
1283: 
1284: \section{Training, Model Selection, and Thresholding}
1285: \label{subsec:experimental_training_protocol}
1286: 
1287: \subsection{Principal Training Configuration}
1288: \label{subsubsec:principal_training_config}
1289: 
1290: Principal architecture and optimisation settings are listed in Table~\ref{tab:experimental_training_config}. Both stages use AdamW, which decouples weight decay from adaptive gradient updates \cite{Loshchilov2019AdamW}, together with focal loss to downweight easy majority-class examples \cite{Lin2017FocalLoss}.
1291: 
1292: \begin{table}[H]
1293:     \centering
1294:     \caption{Principal optimisation configuration. Values changed in a named ablation are the only exceptions.}
1295:     \label{tab:experimental_training_config}
1296:     \begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.0cm}p{4.6cm}p{5.7cm}@{}}
1297:         \toprule
1298:         Setting & Stage~1 & Stage~2 \\
1299:         \midrule
1300:         Architecture and input & Scheme C; $L=128$; head selection; $d=128$ & Target-query gated attention; source-host; $W=128$; $d=128$ \\
1301:         Batch size & 128 & 128 \\
1302:         Optimiser & AdamW & AdamW \\
1303:         Learning rate & $1\times10^{-4}$ & $3\times10^{-5}$ \\
1304:         Weight decay & $1\times10^{-4}$ & $3\times10^{-4}$ \\
1305:         % Gradient clipping & Global norm 1.0 & Global norm 0.5 \\
1306:         Loss & Focal, $\gamma=1$, $\boldsymbol{\alpha}=[1,1.25]$, no label smoothing & Focal, $\gamma=1$, $\boldsymbol{\alpha}=[1,1.01]$, no label smoothing \\
1307:         Sampler target & 10\% class-1 examples & 5\% class-1 examples \\
1308:         Maximum epochs & 500 & 500 \\
1309:         Early-stopping patience & 25 epochs & 15 epochs \\
1310:         Checkpoint criterion & Validation PR-AUC & Validation PR-AUC \\
1311:         % Random seed & 130 & 130 \\
1312:         \bottomrule
1313:     \end{tabular}
1314: \end{table}
1315: 
1316: Weighted sampling with replacement is restricted to training mini-batches. Validation, test, and embedding-export loaders preserve exactly one row per flow and do not sample, preventing duplicated embeddings or changes to the empirical evaluation distribution. CUDA runs use automatic mixed precision. Stage~1 lowers its learning rate after a plateau in validation PR-AUC, whereas Stage~2 follows five warm-up epochs with cosine annealing.
1317: 
1318: \subsection{Checkpoint and Decision-Threshold Selection}
1319: \label{subsubsec:experimental_thresholding}
1320: 
1321: Checkpoint and decision-threshold selection are separate operations applied uniformly across experiments. First, the checkpoint with the highest validation PR-AUC is restored. Validation predictions from that checkpoint then determine the class-1 threshold:
1322: \begin{equation}
1323:     \delta^{*}
1324:     =\arg\max_{\delta\in\Delta}
1325:       F_{1,1}^{\mathrm{val}}(\delta).
1326:     \label{eq:validation_threshold_selection}
1327: \end{equation}
1328: In Stage~1, $\Delta$ contains thresholds induced by the validation precision--recall curve; Stage~2 searches 199 equally spaced values between 0.01 and 0.99. Once selected, $\delta^{*}$ is fixed and transferred to the test partition without adjustment. Test observations play no role in checkpoint, threshold, or hyperparameter selection.
1329: 
1330: \section{Evaluation Metrics}
1331: \label{subsec:experimental_metrics}
1332: 
1333: Metric calculations follow scikit-learn definitions \cite{Pedregosa2011ScikitLearn}. The alert-derived positive class is label~1 and the negative class label~0. Confusion matrices use the fixed orientation
1334: \begin{equation}
1335:     \mathbf{C}=
1336:     \begin{bmatrix}
1337:         \mathrm{TN} & \mathrm{FP}\\
1338:         \mathrm{FN} & \mathrm{TP}
1339:     \end{bmatrix},
1340:     \label{eq:experimental_confusion_matrix}
1341: \end{equation}
1342: with true labels on rows and predictions on columns. Fixing this convention removes ambiguity when matrices are transferred between software packages.
1343: 
1344: \subsection{Class-Wise and Macro-Averaged Measures}
1345: \label{subsubsec:macro_metrics}
1346: 
1347: For $c\in\{0,1\}$, class-wise precision, recall, and F1 are
1348: \begin{align}
1349:     P_c &= \frac{\mathrm{TP}_c}
1350:     {\mathrm{TP}_c+\mathrm{FP}_c}, \\
1351:     R_c &= \frac{\mathrm{TP}_c}
1352:     {\mathrm{TP}_c+\mathrm{FN}_c}, \\
1353:     F_{1,c} &= \frac{2P_cR_c}{P_c+R_c}.
1354:     \label{eq:classwise_metrics}
1355: \end{align}
1356: Undefined ratios take the value zero, consistent with the implementation's
1357: \texttt{zero\_division=0} setting. The macro-averaged metrics are
1358: \begin{align}
1359:     P_{\mathrm{macro}} &= \frac{P_0+P_1}{2}, \\
1360:     R_{\mathrm{macro}} &= \frac{R_0+R_1}{2}, \\
1361:     F_{1,\mathrm{macro}} &= \frac{F_{1,0}+F_{1,1}}{2}.
1362:     \label{eq:experimental_macro_metrics}
1363: \end{align}
1364: 
1365: Macro-F1 is the primary F1 measure and is abbreviated to ``F1'' in high-level tables. It assigns equal weight to the negative and positive classes despite their unequal prevalence; macro-precision and macro-recall are interpreted analogously. To reveal error changes hidden by macro averaging, $P_1$, $R_1$, and $F_{1,1}$ are reported alongside these summaries.
1366: 
1367: Accuracy and weighted averages remain secondary: an always-negative rule would score approximately $96.055\%$ accuracy on the raw data while detecting no positive flow. Neither high accuracy nor weighted F1 alone is therefore sufficient evidence of effective intrusion detection.
1368: 
1369: \subsection{False-Positive Rate and Operational Error Counts}
1370: \label{subsubsec:fpr_metric}
1371: 
1372: False-positive rate is defined as
1373: \begin{equation}
1374:     \mathrm{FPR}=\frac{\mathrm{FP}}{\mathrm{FP}+\mathrm{TN}},
1375:     \label{eq:false_positive_rate}
1376: \end{equation}
1377: and false-negative rate as
1378: \begin{equation}
1379:     \mathrm{FNR}=\frac{\mathrm{FN}}{\mathrm{FN}+\mathrm{TP}}
1380:     =1-R_1.
1381:     \label{eq:false_negative_rate}
1382: \end{equation}
1383: Even a low FPR can generate substantial alert volume in a high-throughput network. It is therefore considered together with absolute FP and FN counts. At the evaluated operating point, a test partition with $N_0$ negative flows produces an expected $N_0\times\mathrm{FPR}$ false alerts.
1384: 
1385: \subsection{Threshold-Independent Ranking Measures}
1386: \label{subsubsec:ranking_metrics}
1387: 
1388: ROC-AUC measures ranking through the true-positive rate over the range of false-positive rates \cite{Fawcett2006ROC}. Under strong class imbalance, however, a large negative population can coexist with a high ROC-AUC. PR-AUC concentrates on retrieval of the positive class and consequently receives greater emphasis here \cite{SaitoRehmsmeier2015}.
1389: 
1390: The reported \texttt{pr\_auc} field is produced by scikit-learn's \texttt{average\_precision\_score}. Thus, it denotes average precision (AP), rather than a trapezoidal area under linearly interpolated precision--recall points:
1391: \begin{equation}
1392:     \mathrm{AP}=\sum_{k}(R_k-R_{k-1})P_k,
1393:     \label{eq:average_precision}
1394: \end{equation}
1395: where $P_k$ and $R_k$ are evaluated at successive score thresholds. Every quantity labelled PR-AUC in the results therefore corresponds to AP. Since random ranking has expected AP near positive prevalence, observed values should be interpreted against the class-1 share of the relevant test manifest.
1396: 
1397: \section{Efficiency and Resource Measurement}
1398: \label{subsec:efficiency_protocol}
1399: 
1400: Experiments ran in Google Colaboratory Pro+ on an NVIDIA L4 GPU with 24~GB memory, using a company-funded allocation of 600 compute units \cite{GoogleColabFAQ2026,NVIDIAL4ProductBrief}. Those units regulate access to hosted resources and do not quantify a fixed amount of GPU computation. Runtime is compared only within the same stage because Stage~1 and Stage~2 differ in data loading, early stopping, and execution path.
1401: 
1402: Trainable parameters are counted directly from tensors that require gradients. Stage~1 wall time includes fitting, validation, and checkpointing. It is treated as a descriptive single-run cost because early stopping and variation in the hosted environment affect duration. For Stage~2 timing, gradients are disabled and the model is placed in evaluation mode. Per-flow latency is calculated as
1403: \begin{equation}
1404:     t_{\mathrm{flow}}=\frac{T_{\mathrm{test}}}{N_{\mathrm{test}}},
1405:     \qquad
1406:     Q=\frac{N_{\mathrm{test}}}{T_{\mathrm{test}}},
1407:     \label{eq:inference_efficiency}
1408: \end{equation}
1409: where $Q$ denotes flows processed per second. Timing starts after Stage~1 embeddings and context indices have been prepared, so it measures model evaluation rather than the complete path from PCAP to alert.
1410: 
1411: \begin{table}[H]
1412:     \centering
1413:     \caption{Execution environment for the reported experiment blocks.}
1414:     \label{tab:experimental_environment}
1415:     \begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.4cm}p{10.0cm}@{}}
1416:         \toprule
1417:         Component & Recorded environment \\
1418:         \midrule
1419:         Hosted platform & \ExpPlatform \\
1420:         Purchased allocation & \ExpComputeBudget \\
1421:         Accelerator & \ExpGPU \\
1422:         Host processor and RAM & \ExpCPU \\
1423:         Operating environment & \ExpOS \\
1424:         Learning software & \ExpSoftware \\
1425:         Traffic extractor & \ExpSuricata \\
1426:         \bottomrule
1427:     \end{tabular}
1428: \end{table}
1429: 
1430: Available run metadata do not retain the exact CPU, software snapshot, Suricata version, ruleset snapshot, configuration, custom-module revision, or PCAP checksum. Exact reproduction of runtime and label generation is consequently limited, and the missing provenance is identified as unavailable rather than reconstructed after the fact.
1431: \providecommand{\resultsfigurepath}{figures}
1432: \IfFileExists{figures/stage1_model_comparison.pdf}{}{%
1433:     \renewcommand{\resultsfigurepath}{MScCS/figures}%
1434: }
1435: 
===== LINES 1436-1582 =====
1436: \chapter{Results and Discussion}
1437: \label{sec:results}
1438: 
1439: Unless explicitly qualified, F1, precision, and recall refer to macro averages; class-1 quantities carry a separate label. Discussion centres on macro-F1, PR-AUC, class-1 performance, FP, FN, and FPR because the majority class dominates accuracy. Numerical differences are interpreted only when the models share a test manifest.
1440: 
1441: \section{Stage~1: Intra-Flow Representation}
1442: \label{subsec:results_stage1}
1443: 
1444: \subsection{Architecture and Temporal-Encoding Effects}
1445: \label{subsubsec:results_stage1_models}
1446: 
1447: Stage~1 model families and encoding ablations are contrasted on a common flow test set in Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage1_models}; their parameter counts and training times appear in Table~\ref{tab:results_stage1_errors_efficiency}.
1448: 
1449: \begin{figure}[H]
1450:     \centering
1451:     \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{\resultsfigurepath/stage1_model_comparison.pdf}
1452:     \caption{Stage~1 architecture and encoding comparison on the common flow test manifest. The horizontal axis begins at 0.52, and absolute values are printed at the end of each bar.}
1453:     \label{fig:results_stage1_models}
1454: \end{figure}
1455: 
1456: Among these models, position+time gives the Transformer the best overall balance: macro-F1 reaches $0.8882$ and PR-AUC $0.8564$. LSTM+time is the strongest non-Transformer alternative by macro-F1, yet the Transformer improves this score by 2.05 percentage points. At the selected operating point, 95 additional positive flows are recovered at the cost of 10 more false positives. The gain therefore reflects a materially different error balance rather than a change in a summary score alone.
1457: 
1458: Encoding ablations separate two meanings of sequence location. Position records where a packet occurs, whereas cumulative time distinguishes a rapid burst from an identical order spread over a longer interval. Time-only encoding exceeds position-only, and combining them adds another 0.64 percentage points of macro-F1. Both FP and FN are also lower than under no encoding. These changes support order and elapsed time as complementary, rather than interchangeable, descriptions of a flow.
1459: 
1460: The lowest FPR, $0.8293\%$, belongs to the GRU, but that operating point misses 437 positive flows. Position+time introduces only 14 additional FP and removes 131 FN relative to the GRU. A low false-alert rate is therefore not sufficient by itself: conservative thresholds can suppress FP by transferring error to the positive class.
1461: 
1462: The flow-statistics MLP provides the direct aggregation-only control. It obtains macro-F1 $0.7762$ and PR-AUC $0.5604$ on the same test composition, compared with $0.8882$ and $0.8564$ for position+time. Its 853 FP and 501 FN fall to 296 and 306 when packet dynamics are modelled. Duration, count, and inter-arrival summaries therefore retain useful global information but fail to reconstruct all of the local order and timing present in the packet sequence.
1463: 
1464: Operating-point errors are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage1_errors}. Because the negative examples are shared, FP bar height is directly proportional to FPR.
1465: 
1466: \begin{figure}[H]
1467:     \centering
1468:     \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{\resultsfigurepath/stage1_error_comparison.pdf}
1469:     \caption{Stage~1 false-positive and false-negative counts on the common flow test manifest. FPR is printed beside each FP count.}
1470:     \label{fig:results_stage1_errors}
1471: \end{figure}
1472: 
1473: LSTM also benefits from explicit time, with reductions in both error types. For CNN, however, the effect is mixed: the selected operating point improves, while PR-AUC declines and FN rises. This disagreement is informative. Thresholded F1 characterises one decision point, whereas PR-AUC evaluates ranking over thresholds. Timing is therefore not beneficial merely because it is present; its representation within the encoder matters, and an appended interval feature is not equivalent to an elapsed-time sequence coordinate.
1474: 
1475: \begin{table}[H]
1476:     \centering
1477:     \caption{Stage~1 parameter counts and single-run training times measured on an NVIDIA L4 GPU in Google Colab.}
1478:     \label{tab:results_stage1_errors_efficiency}
1479:     \begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
1480:         \toprule
1481:         Model & Trainable parameters & Training time (s) & Training time (min) \\
1482:         \midrule
1483:         Position+time Transformer & 344,963 & 4,337.6 & 72.29 \\
1484:         Time-only Transformer & 344,963 & 2,250.5 & 37.51 \\
1485:         Position-only Transformer & 344,963 & 1,994.7 & 33.25 \\
1486:         LSTM+time & 217,346 & 3,556.3 & 59.27 \\
1487:         CNN1D+time & 70,274 & 1,162.4 & 19.37 \\
1488:         GRU & 162,690 & 3,684.4 & 61.41 \\
1489:         LSTM & 216,834 & 3,381.2 & 56.35 \\
1490:         CNN1D & 68,354 & 1,591.0 & 26.52 \\
1491:         BiLSTM & 564,738 & 2,625.7 & 43.76 \\
1492:         No-encoding Transformer & 344,963 & 2,213.9 & 36.90 \\
1493:         \bottomrule
1494:     \end{tabular}
1495: \end{table}
1496: 
1497: No MLP efficiency entry is shown in Table~\ref{tab:results_stage1_errors_efficiency}, as its parameter count and training time were not retained.
1498: 
1499: CNN1D+time records the shortest Stage~1 training time, 1,162.4 seconds; position+time requires 4,337.6 seconds. Despite the BiLSTM's larger parameter count, the proposed encoder is the most expensive run observed. Each value was collected on an NVIDIA L4 GPU, but it includes early stopping, data loading, and variation in the hosted runtime. These measurements describe total training cost, not architecture-only throughput.
1500: 
1501: \subsection{Packet and Flow-Statistics Integration}
1502: \label{subsubsec:results_stage1_fusion}
1503: 
1504: The fusion comparison in Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage1_fusion} uses one shared flow manifest. Scheme~A tiles the statistical vector across packet tokens, Scheme~B retains packet inputs alone, and Scheme~C conditions packet representations through bounded token-FiLM from a separate branch.
1505: 
1506: \begin{figure}[H]
1507:     \centering
1508:     \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{\resultsfigurepath/stage1_fusion_comparison.pdf}
1509:     \caption{Controlled Stage~1 integration comparison on the common flow manifest. Left: macro metrics and threshold-independent ranking metrics. Right: false-positive and false-negative counts. The score axis is truncated and explicitly labelled.}
1510:     \label{fig:results_stage1_fusion}
1511: \end{figure}
1512: 
1513: Removing the tiled vector in Scheme~B raises macro-F1 by 1.30 percentage points and PR-AUC by 2.38 points over Scheme~A, although Scheme~A retains a small macro-precision advantage. Repetition of one aggregate vector at every position may therefore interfere with distinctions among packet tokens. Scheme~C strengthens this interpretation: flow statistics appear more useful when encoded through a dedicated pathway than when copied into each packet position.
1514: 
1515: Scheme~C is strongest overall, with macro-F1 $0.8955$, PR-AUC $0.8763$, 267 FP, and 293 FN. Both error counts improve over packet-only Scheme~B, indicating that aggregate statistics add information when they condition rather than replace packet evidence. Attribution must nevertheless remain at the level of the integrated design. Scheme~C introduces both extra parameters and a separate statistical pathway, so the present ablation cannot assign the entire gain specifically to token-FiLM. A parameter-matched concatenation control would be needed for that narrower claim.
1516: 
1517: \subsection{Sequence Length and Packet Selection}
1518: \label{subsubsec:results_sequence_length}
1519: 
1520: Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage1_sensitivity} presents both sensitivity analyses: sequence length is traced in the line panel, and selection policies are compared through grouped precision and recall bars.
1521: 
1522: \begin{figure}[H]
1523:     \centering
1524:     \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]
1525:     {\resultsfigurepath/stage1_sensitivity.pdf}
1526:     \caption{Stage~1 sensitivity analysis. Left: maximum packet-sequence length under head selection. Right: packet-selection strategy at $L=128$. Dashed lines identify the selected sequence length. Truncated axes are explicitly labelled.}
1527:     \label{fig:results_stage1_sensitivity}
1528: \end{figure}
1529: 
1530: Longer sequences do not produce monotonic improvement. Ranking is weaker when only a few early packets are available, implying that those packets are not always sufficient for discrimination. Performance peaks at $L=128$ by macro-F1 and overall error balance. Increasing the budget to 256 adds 46 FP and lowers both macro-F1 and PR-AUC; additional packets can therefore add volume without adding useful evidence.
1531: 
1532: Aggregate sweep results do not identify the mechanism behind this peak. Changing $L$ affects long truncated flows but leaves sufficiently short flows unchanged, so dilution by later packets cannot be separated from flow-length composition or optimisation effects. Such an explanation would require length-stratified evaluation. Consequently, $L=128$ is treated as the best observed budget for this capture, not a universal horizon.
1533: 
1534: At $L=128$, the macro-F1 difference between head and head-tail is only 0.16 percentage points. With one seed, this margin is treated as practically inconclusive. Random selection shifts the detector towards lower FPR and more FN. Head remains the principal policy because it is deterministic, provides the best observed macro-F1 and attack-class recall, and preserves a contiguous early prefix without waiting for the flow's final packets. Its small advantage over head-tail is not interpreted as a robust ordering.
1535: 
1536: \section{Stage~2: Inter-Flow Context}
1537: \label{subsec:results_stage2}
1538: 
1539: \subsection{Gain over Independent Flow Classification}
1540: \label{subsubsec:results_hierarchical_gain}
1541: 
1542: The principal cross-stage comparison appears in Table~\ref{tab:results_hierarchical_gain}. Both rows share the same test flows: one classifies each Scheme~C embedding independently, and the other augments that frozen representation with causal source-host history.
1543: 
1544: \begin{table}[H]
1545:     \centering
1546:     \caption{Independent Stage~1 classification and the complete hierarchical system on the common full-data manifest.}
1547:     \label{tab:results_hierarchical_gain}
1548:     \resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{%
1549:     \begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrr@{}}
1550:         \toprule
1551:         Model & $P_{\mathrm{macro}}$ & $R_{\mathrm{macro}}$ &
1552:         $F_{1,\mathrm{macro}}$ & $F_{1,1}$ & ROC-AUC & PR-AUC & FPR (\%) \\
1553:         \midrule
1554:         Stage~1 Scheme C & 0.8990 & 0.8919 & 0.8954 & 0.7994 & 0.9890 &
1555:         0.8763 & 0.8249 \\
1556:         Stage~2 source-host & \textbf{0.9328} & \textbf{0.9224} &
1557:         \textbf{0.9275} & \textbf{0.8610} & \textbf{0.9938} &
1558:         \textbf{0.9345} & \textbf{0.5429} \\
1559:         \bottomrule
1560:     \end{tabular}%
1561:     }
1562: \end{table}
1563: 
1564: Adding Stage~2 raises macro-F1 by 3.21 percentage points and PR-AUC by 5.82 points relative to independent Scheme~C classification. The contextual system removes 228 FP and 205 FN, a relative FPR reduction of approximately 34.2\%. Since both error types decline, the gain is not an accuracy artefact or a simple shift towards one class. This comparison concerns the hierarchy as an integrated system. Subsequent architecture controls test simpler encoders, but the capacity mismatch of the no-context head prevents the full difference from being attributed solely to historical evidence.
1565: 
1566: 
1567: \subsection{Which Relation Defines Useful Context?}
1568: \label{subsubsec:results_context_relation}
1569: 
1570: Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage2_context} links ranking quality to operating behaviour. Macro-F1 and PR-AUC are shown on the left; class-1 recall and FPR locate each relation on the right.
1571: 
1572: \begin{figure}[H]
1573:     \centering
1574:     \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{\resultsfigurepath/stage2_context_analysis.pdf}
1575:     \caption{Stage~2 context-relation ablation at $W=128$. Left: macro-F1 and PR-AUC. Right: class-1 recall versus FPR, where the desirable region is the upper-left corner.}
1576:     \label{fig:results_stage2_context}
1577: \end{figure}
1578: 
1579: Source-host history yields the strongest balance: macro-F1 is $0.9275$, PR-AUC $0.9345$, and FPR is the lowest among the relations. Repeated outbound activity from one initiator may preserve evidence of scanning, retries, or coordinated connections. Global recency, by comparison, mixes unrelated flows, while destination history may combine many benign clients of a widely used service. This interpretation agrees with relational and window-level findings in prior NIDS work \cite{Deng2023FlowTopology,MartinReizabal2026FlowWindow}, but it remains a mechanism hypothesis because binary labels do not identify the attack families responsible for the gain.
1580: 
1581: Endpoint context operates at a different point: fewer positives are missed, but more false alerts are produced. Matching either endpoint in either role can retrieve lateral or bidirectional activity, while also admitting a more heterogeneous neighbourhood. It may be appropriate when FN carry a higher operational cost; under the balanced criterion used here, source-host remains preferable. Time-only context ranks flows less effectively and produces more FN, suggesting that chronological proximity without an entity relation is insufficiently selective for this capture.
