   45: \supervisor{Le Viet Duc}
   46: \covertitle{A Hierarchical Time-Aware Transformer for Flow-Level Network Intrusion Detection}
   47: \doctype{Final Project}
   48: 
   49: %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
   50: 
   51: \begin{document}
   52: \makecoverpage
   53: \tableofcontents
   54: \begin{abstract}
   55: Many flow-level network intrusion detection pipelines reduce each connection to aggregate measurements and then classify flows in isolation. This efficient abstraction suppresses packet ordering and non-uniform timing, and it can obscure evidence that becomes visible only across related connections. To retain both levels of structure, this thesis develops a hierarchical detector that does not consume payload content. Aligned packet records and flow statistics are extracted from an enterprise PCAP through a custom Suricata output module. Stage~1 combines positional and cumulative-time information when encoding the ordered packet metadata, conditions the packet tokens on flow statistics, and exports a fused representation for each flow. In Stage~2, the target representation queries a start-time-ordered history of earlier related flow embeddings before the final prediction is produced.
   56: 
   57: Chronological partitions are used throughout the evaluation. Because positive flows are scarce, the analysis gives priority to macro-F1, PR-AUC, attack-class performance, confusion matrices, and false-positive rate. On the controlled Stage~1 flow test manifest, the complete fused representation reaches a macro-F1 of 0.8954 and a PR-AUC of 0.8763, exceeding the aggregate-flow, recurrent, convolutional, and encoding controls. On the separate Stage~2 flow manifest, source-host context raises macro-F1 from 0.8954 to 0.9275 and PR-AUC from 0.8763 to 0.9345 while reducing both types of classification error. The target-query model also surpasses the no-context, vanilla Transformer, LSTM, GRU, and CNN--LSTM alternatives, with model-level latency comparable to that of the vanilla Transformer. External evaluation uses five non-overlapping 20,000-flow windows from the public CICIDS2017 Wednesday capture and a 38,513-flow company capture obtained from a different server. Pooled macro-F1 across the five public windows is 0.8966, while the separate-server capture reaches 0.9026. Taken together, the results indicate that explicit intra-flow timing and selective inter-flow context supply complementary and partly transferable evidence, although performance varies across external time windows.
   58: 
   59:   \begin{keywords}
   60:    Network intrusion detection, packet sequence, time-aware Transformer, flow representation, cross-flow context, class imbalance
   61:   \end{keywords}
   62: \end{abstract}
   63: 
   64: %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
   65: 
   66: \chapter{Introduction}
   67: \label{sec:introduction}
   68: 
   69: Enterprise systems, cloud platforms, mobile networks, and Internet-of-Things (IoT) deployments collectively produce large and heterogeneous traffic streams. Continuous monitoring is needed to identify activities such as reconnaissance, exploitation attempts, botnet communication, distributed denial-of-service (DDoS), and multi-stage intrusion campaigns. Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDSs) consequently form an important layer of cyber-security infrastructure \cite{Roesch1999,SommerPaxson2010}. Their practical design nevertheless remains difficult. Contemporary attacks may be distributed, adaptive, and temporally extended, while encryption restricts direct payload inspection and increases reliance on headers, metadata, and behavioural traffic characteristics \cite{Sharma2025EncryptedSurvey,Dong2025EncryptedPretrain}.
   70: 
   71: Operational deployments continue to rely on signature engines such as Snort and Suricata because their rules are interpretable, computationally efficient, and effective against known threats \cite{Roesch1999,SuricataGuide2026}. Suspicious traffic is recognised through patterns designed and maintained by domain experts. Such rules remain valuable, but their coverage is necessarily bounded: unseen attacks and evolving variants may not match an existing signature, and malicious intent may emerge only from temporal or contextual regularities spanning several flows \cite{SommerPaxson2010,Hozouri2025Survey}. Rule-based analysis alone is therefore unlikely to provide sufficient coverage as traffic volumes and attack behaviours continue to diversify.
   72: 
   73: Learning-based NIDSs seek greater adaptability by estimating decision boundaries from observed traffic rather than depending exclusively on predefined rules. In conventional pipelines, a bidirectional flow is represented by aggregate features such as its duration, packet and byte counts, rates, and inter-arrival-time statistics \cite{BuczakGuven2016,Ahmed2016Survey}. Compact fixed-length vectors support efficient large-scale processing, but the compression removes detail about the process that generated each flow. Packet order, bursts, directional transitions, and uneven temporal gaps can all disappear during aggregation. For attacks whose distinguishing evidence lies in how a connection unfolds rather than only in its total volume, that information loss is consequential.
   74: 
   75: Richer traffic representations have been investigated with convolutional and recurrent neural networks, LSTMs, GRUs, and autoencoder-based architectures \cite{Yin2017RNNIDS,Shone2018NNAE,Mirsky2018Kitsune}. These models can capture non-linear feature interactions while reducing manual feature engineering. Yet their inputs often remain aggregate flow records. In some sequence-based studies, the sequence is formed from fixed-size flow attributes rather than the packets observed within a connection. Such formulations preserve only part of the natural hierarchy of network traffic: packets constitute flows, and related flows in turn express host- or endpoint-level behaviour.
   76: 
   77: Self-attention offers a suitable mechanism for addressing this hierarchy because distant sequence elements can interact directly and training can be parallelised \cite{Vaswani2017}. Transformer-based systems have consequently achieved competitive results in traffic classification and intrusion detection \cite{Wu2022RTIDS,Han2023GTID,Manocchio2024FlowTransformer}. Time-aware variants show, in particular, that detection can benefit when order and elapsed time are encoded as distinct signals \cite{Han2023GTID}; FlowTransformer demonstrates the architectural flexibility available for flow-based NIDS evaluation \cite{Manocchio2024FlowTransformer}. Even so, a substantial portion of this literature continues to represent each flow as one flat item. Packet-level dynamics inside a flow and contextual dependencies between flows are seldom modelled together.
   78: 
   79: To address this gap, the thesis organises detection into two stages. Raw PCAP traffic is first converted into aligned packet records and flow statistics by a custom Suricata output module. Stage~1 derives a flow representation from packet order, elapsed time, and aggregate measurements. A bounded causal history of related representations is then used by Stage~2 to refine the flow-level decision. Separating these operations permits intra-flow representation learning and inter-flow reasoning to be assessed independently without changing the final prediction granularity.
   80: 
   81: The central research problem of this thesis is therefore:
   82: 
   83: \begin{displayquote}
   84: How can network intrusion detection benefit from packet-level temporal modelling and cross-flow contextual modelling while preserving the hierarchical structure of network traffic?
   85: \end{displayquote}
   86: 
   87: To answer this question, the thesis studies two research questions:
   88: 
   89: \begin{itemize}
   90:     \item \textbf{RQ1:} Does time-aware packet-sequence modelling produce more informative flow representations than flow-level aggregation alone?
   91:     \item \textbf{RQ2:} Does incorporating cross-flow context improve flow-level intrusion detection compared with independent per-flow classification?
   92: \end{itemize}
   93: 
   94: The thesis makes three principal contributions:
   95: 
   96: \begin{itemize}
   97:     \item An aligned PCAP-to-learning pipeline built around a custom Suricata output module, with explicit separation among predictive variables, context identifiers, and alert-derived targets.
   98:     \item A Stage~1 representation that integrates packet position, cumulative elapsed time, mask-aware self-attention, and bounded flow-conditioned token modulation.
   99:     \item A causal Stage~2 target-query attention model that draws evidence from relation-specific histories while excluding future flows and historical labels.
  100:     \item External evaluation on chronological windows from the public CICIDS2017 Wednesday capture and on a separate company-provided server capture, complementing the source-domain chronological test.
  101:     % \item A controlled evaluation of representation, fusion, sequence length, context relation, context capacity, architecture, and computational cost under class imbalance.
  102: \end{itemize}
  103: 
  104: Given the low prevalence of the positive class, macro-F1, PR-AUC, attack-class performance, and false-positive rate carry more interpretive weight than accuracy alone.
  105: 
  106: \chapter{Related Work}
  107: \label{sec:related_work}
  108: 
  109: Research on network intrusion detection spans manually specified signatures and data-driven models learned from packet traces, flow records, or ordered network events. The literature can be organised along two dimensions: representation granularity and contextual scope. Inputs range from packet bytes and header sequences to aggregate flow statistics and learned embeddings. Most systems assign labels independently to individual packets or flows; comparatively fewer studies represent temporal or relational dependencies across several flows.
  110: 
  225: \chapter{Dataset Construction and Preprocessing}
  226: \label{sec:dataset_preprocessing}
  227: 
  228: Two synchronised views of the traffic are required: an ordered set of packet records for every flow and a single statistical record describing that flow. The raw CSV schema is kept distinct from the Stage~1 predictor set and from identifiers used only to construct Stage~2 context, since linkage metadata need not be suitable predictive variables. Alignment and sequence assembly are deterministic, whereas every transformation estimated from data is fitted on the training partition. This organisation supports reproducibility and limits information leakage during NIDS evaluation \cite{Shiravi2012DatasetGeneration,Ring2019Datasets,Goldschmidt2025Datasets,Pinto2025DatasetReview}.
  229: 
  230: \section{Data Provenance and Scope}
  231: \label{subsec:data_provenance}
  232: 
  233: Experiments are conducted on \datasetfield{ar002_et12_20260511_001.pcap}, an approximately $8.62\,\mathrm{GB}$ packet capture supplied by the company. Organisational staff acquired the trace from the company network with an internal traffic-collection tool; no laboratory traffic was generated for this thesis. CICIDS-style datasets schedule benign profiles and attacks under controlled conditions \cite{Sharafaldin2018CICIDS}. By contrast, the present trace records enterprise traffic as it was visible at the collection point. This improves environmental realism but also binds the observed traffic mix, host population, sensor visibility, and attack coverage to one organisation and capture period.
  234: 
  235: Because the dataset is proprietary, it is not presented as a public benchmark. Sensitive network identifiers, notably IP addresses and ports, remain in the source PCAP and CSV files; only aggregate quantities are reported in the thesis. Before any artefact leaves the authorised environment, host identifiers should be replaced consistently, superfluous endpoint fields removed, and the result reviewed under company data-governance policy. Stage~2 relies on equality relations between hosts, so stable pseudonyms are preferable to removing every identifier, although the pseudonym map itself need not be disclosed. Raw addresses are still retained by the current extraction code in local metadata. Pseudonymisation is therefore a requirement for future release, not a property claimed for the current CSV files.
  236: 
  237: \section{External Evaluation Datasets}
  238: \label{subsec:external_datasets}
  239: 
  240: Two datasets that are disjoint from the source capture are used to examine transfer beyond its internal chronological test. The first is the publicly available CICIDS2017 Wednesday-workingHours capture, corresponding to Wednesday, 5 July 2017 \cite{Sharafaldin2018CICIDS}. CICIDS2017 is not company-provided traffic and is treated as a public cross-dataset test. Compatible Stage~1 external-test artefacts are ordered chronologically and divided into multiple non-overlapping windows of 20,000 flows. To preserve the qualitative class-imbalance condition of the source task, external windows are retained when label~1 remains the minority class and label~0 the majority class. Under this prevalence-based criterion, the evaluated windows are \texttt{w01}, \texttt{w02}, \texttt{w06}, \texttt{w07}, and \texttt{w08}, contributing 100,000 flows in total. The criterion concerns class composition rather than model performance. Window-level reporting is retained because aggregate performance alone can conceal temporal variation in false-positive and false-negative behaviour.
  241: 
  242: The second external dataset, \datasetfield{firewall_test_20250731_160700}, was supplied by the company but collected from a server environment different from that of \datasetfield{ar002_et12_20260511_001}. It contains 38,513 evaluated flows and is used as a cross-server organisational test. It is neither part of CICIDS2017 nor described as a public benchmark. This distinction separates public cross-dataset transfer from transfer to a different server within the organisational setting.
  243: 
  244: The external datasets are evaluation resources rather than extensions of the source training partition. Their class prevalence differs from that of the source capture: the five prevalence-compatible CICIDS2017 windows contain between 13.34\% and 18.20\% positive flows, whereas the separate-server capture contains 3.285\%. Metrics are consequently interpreted within each dataset rather than through accuracy alone. Selecting CICIDS2017 windows in which label~1 remains the minority improves comparability with the imbalanced source task, but it also limits the external claim to windows satisfying that condition rather than the complete Wednesday capture. Differences in capture environment, traffic composition, labelling provenance, and class balance mean that an external score is evidence of transfer under the evaluated pipeline, not proof of invariant performance across arbitrary networks.
  245: 
  246: \section{PCAP-to-CSV Extraction}
  247: \label{subsec:pcap_extraction}
  248: 
  249: Suricata, an open-source network threat detection engine, is used to replay the PCAP \cite{SuricataGuide2026}. The custom C module at \datasetfield{suricata-plugin/custom-out.c} registers separate packet and flow loggers through Suricata's low-level output interfaces. Both CSV views are emitted during the same pass. Consequently, packet-to-flow membership does not need to be reconstructed by another tool, and the packet and flow tables retain a common bidirectional \datasetfield{flow_id}.
  250: 
  251: The construction path is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:data_construction_pipeline}. Packet rows retain microsecond timestamps and their order within each flow. In parallel, online aggregates are maintained by the flow logger and written once a flow completes. Broad feature families follow CICFlowMeter/CICIDS-style data, with additional indicators recording whether initial TCP-window measurements are available in the forward and backward directions
  252: \cite{Sharafaldin2018CICIDS}.
  253: 
