  533:         \datasetfield{tcp_rst}, \datasetfield{tcp_psh},
  534:         \datasetfield{tcp_ack_flag}, \datasetfield{tcp_urg},
  535:         \datasetfield{tcp_ece}, \datasetfield{tcp_cwr} \\
  536:         \bottomrule
  537:     \end{tabular}
  538: \end{table}
  539: 
  540: Stage~1 excludes raw IP addresses and ports, packet and flow identifiers, absolute timestamps, TCP sequence and acknowledgement numbers, the combined TCP-flag integer, \datasetfield{l7_proto}, and both targets. Their removal discourages memorisation of site-specific endpoints and prevents identifiers or capture position from becoming target shortcuts. Relative timing is retained separately through a tensor derived from \datasetfield{flow_iat_us}; the absolute packet timestamp is not supplied as an ordinary feature.
  541: 
  542: When statistical fusion is enabled, a configured subset of Table~\ref{tab:flow_schema} is processed through a separate branch. The principal subset covers duration, directional packet and byte totals, length distributions, rates, inter-arrival summaries, flag counts, header lengths, segment and window measurements, and selected active-period statistics. Availability of the two initial-window measurements is represented by binary indicators. Endpoint identities and ports, absolute flow times, and \datasetfield{label} are omitted. Since the packet-only ablation disables this branch, presence in the raw schema does not imply use in every experiment.
  543: 
  544: Three metadata fields accompany every Stage~1 embedding: \datasetfield{flow_start_timestamp_us}, \datasetfield{source_ip}, and \datasetfield{destination_ip}. They define relations for Stage~2 and are not Stage~1 predictors. Host equality determines source-host, destination-host, and endpoint contexts, while start time establishes ordering. Addresses are never one-hot encoded as semantic features, and historical labels are not retrieved into context. The required relations are thereby preserved without directly training on host identity.
  545: 
  546: \section{Train-Fitted Feature Transformations}
  547: \label{subsec:feature_transformations}
  548: 
  549: Every data-dependent transformation is estimated from the training partition and subsequently applied unchanged to validation and test records. Estimating clipping limits, scaling statistics, or category vocabularies from the complete dataset would expose information from later partitions \cite{Arp2022DosDonts}. Packet and flow variables follow pipelines of the same form, but their fitted parameters remain separate.
  550: 
  551: \paragraph{Numerical features.}
  552: After conversion to numeric form, non-finite entries are marked as missing. Selected nonnegative variables with heavy-tailed distributions undergo the transform
  553: \begin{equation}
  554:     \widetilde{x}=\log(1+\max(x,0)).
  555:     \label{eq:log1p_preprocessing}
  556: \end{equation}
  557: Configured variables include packet and payload lengths, TCP window, header length, UDP length, and numerous flow-level counts, durations, byte totals, rates, and inter-arrival summaries. Zero imputation is applied after the logarithmic transform.
  558: 
  559: For numerical variable $j$, lower and upper clipping limits are the training quantiles $q_{0.001,j}^{\mathrm{tr}}$ and $q_{0.999,j}^{\mathrm{tr}}$. Winsorisation in every partition is defined by
  560: \begin{equation}
  561:     x_{ij}^{\mathrm{clip}}
  562:     = \min\!\left(
  563:         \max\!\left(\widetilde{x}_{ij},q_{0.001,j}^{\mathrm{tr}}\right),
  564:         q_{0.999,j}^{\mathrm{tr}}
  565:       \right).
  566:     \label{eq:quantile_clipping}
  567: \end{equation}
  568: Robust scaling is then applied in the principal configuration:
  569: \begin{equation}
  570:     x_{ij}^{\mathrm{scaled}}
  571:     = \frac{x_{ij}^{\mathrm{clip}}-
  572:              \operatorname{median}_{\mathrm{tr}}(x_j)}
  573:             {\operatorname{IQR}_{\mathrm{tr}}(x_j)},
  574:     \label{eq:robust_scaling}
  575: \end{equation}
  576: where the denominator is computed from the training interquartile range. Logarithmic compression, quantile clipping, and robust scaling limit the influence of extreme bursts without drawing statistics from the test partition.
  577: 
  578: \paragraph{Categorical features.}
  579: An explicit \datasetfield{MISSING} token represents absent categorical values. The one-hot vocabulary is learned from training categories; categories encountered only during validation or testing are ignored, leaving the input dimension unchanged.
  580: 
  581: \paragraph{Binary features.}
  582: Binary fields are first converted to numbers. Missing and non-finite entries become zero, as do all non-positive values, while positive entries become one. No one-hot encoding, logarithmic transform, clipping, or scaling is applied to these indicators.
  583: 
  584: \section{Packet-Sequence Construction}
  585: \label{subsec:sequence_construction}
  586: 
  587: Packets in aligned flow $f$ are sorted by \datasetfield{timestamp_us}. Under the principal \datasetfield{head} policy, at most $L=128$ packets are retained. Flows longer than $L$ contribute their first 128 observations; shorter sequences are padded with zeros on the right. Valid positions are recorded in a Boolean mask so that padding is excluded from attention and pooling. The resulting tensor shapes are
  588: \begin{equation}
  589:     \mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times L\times d_p},\qquad
  590:     \mathbf{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times L},\qquad
  591:     \mathbf{M}\in\{0,1\}^{N\times L},
  592:     \label{eq:stage1_tensor_shapes}
  593: \end{equation}
  594: with $d_p$ denoting the transformed packet-feature dimension after one-hot encoding.
  595: 
  596: Nonnegative within-flow inter-arrival times define the timing tensor:
  597: \begin{equation}
  598:     T_{f,i}=\log\!\left(1+\max(\Delta t_{f,i},0)\right),
  599:     \qquad
  600:     \Delta t_{f,i}=\datasetfield{flow_iat_us}(p_{f,i}).
  601:     \label{eq:time_tensor}
  602: \end{equation}
  603: This tensor enters the time-aware encoder separately; absolute timestamps are not concatenated to the packet features.
  604: 
  605: Two additional policies support the truncation ablation. \datasetfield{head_tail} combines the first $\lfloor L/2\rfloor$ packets with the final $L-\lfloor L/2\rfloor$ packets. \datasetfield{random} samples $L$ positions without replacement from a deterministic seed derived from \datasetfield{flow_id} and the global seed, after which the selected packets are returned to chronological order. Separate experiments consider $L\in\{8,16,32,64,128,256\}$. Only the retained evidence and associated computation change; flow membership in each split remains fixed.
  606: 
  607: \section{Chronological Train--Validation--Test Partition}
  608: \label{subsec:chronological_partition}
  609: 
  610: Unique flows are stably ordered by \datasetfield{flow_start_timestamp_us} before a flow-level split is made. The earliest 70\% form the training partition, the following 10\% form validation, and the final 20\% are reserved for testing. With \datasetfield{stratify=false}, these chronological boundaries are not adjusted to equalise class proportions. For the $421{,}898$ aligned records, this procedure yields the partition sizes in Table~\ref{tab:chronological_split}.
  611: 
  612: \begin{table}[H]
  613:     \centering
  614:     \caption{Principal chronological flow partition.}
  615:     \label{tab:chronological_split}
  616:     \begin{tabular}{@{}lrrl@{}}
  617:         \toprule
  618:         Partition & Ratio & Number of flows & Temporal role \\
  619:         \midrule
  620:         Training & 70\% & 295,329 & Earliest flows \\
  621:         Validation & 10\% & 42,189 & Intermediate flows \\
  622:         Test & 20\% & 84,380 & Latest flows \\
  623:         \midrule
  624:         Total & 100\% & 421,898 & \\
  625:         \bottomrule
  626:     \end{tabular}
  627: \end{table}
  628: 
  629: Every packet follows the partition assignment of its parent \datasetfield{flow_id}; no flow can span two partitions. Only training-flow packets and statistics are used to fit preprocessing. Validation data support hyperparameter choice, early stopping, and decision-threshold selection, while final evaluation is confined to the test partition. Relative to random row splitting, chronological separation more closely reflects deployment on later traffic and reduces leakage from distributing related packets or temporally adjacent behaviour across partitions \cite{SommerPaxson2010,Arp2022DosDonts}.
  630: 
  631: \section{Stage~1 Artifacts Supplied to Stage~2}
  632: \label{subsec:stage2_artifacts}
  633: 
  634: Once Stage~1 has been trained, exactly one embedding and one metadata record are exported for each flow in every partition. Metadata comprise \datasetfield{flow_id}, the target, partition name, \datasetfield{flow_start_timestamp_us}, and source and destination identifiers. The embedding provides Stage~2 content; time and host identity are used only to determine which earlier flows are eligible. Retaining the partition name guards against unintended context construction across split boundaries. Targets remain available for training and evaluation but never become context features.
  635: 
  636: Each Stage~1 run also records the selected features, encoder categories, clipping limits, scaling parameters, sequence policy, random seed, ordered flow identifiers, partition-level class counts, model configuration, and checkpoint identity. These records link every Stage~2 result to the Stage~1 checkpoint and split manifest from which its embeddings originated.
  637: 
  638: \section{Validity, Privacy, and Reproducibility Considerations}
  639: \label{subsec:dataset_limitations}
  640: 
  641: Although the resulting dataset supports evaluation on a large, temporally ordered enterprise trace, four constraints delimit the conclusions.
  642: 
  643: \begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{L\arabic*:},leftmargin=*]
  644:     \item \textbf{Alert-derived ground truth.} Label coverage and error reflect a single Suricata ruleset. A negative record is therefore more accurately interpreted as ``no alert observed'' than as independently verified benign traffic.
  645: 
  646:     \item \textbf{Limited multi-source scope.} Model development and ablation evidence comes from one company source capture. The public CICIDS2017 windows and separate-server company capture extend evaluation beyond that source, but two external sources cannot establish generalisation across arbitrary organisations, sensors, rulesets, or extended periods.
  647: 
  648:     \item \textbf{Capture visibility.} Only traffic observable at the collection point appears in the PCAP. Packet loss, asymmetric routing, encryption, and incomplete flows may change the extracted measurements. Where organisational policy allows, documentation should include the collection tool, interface location, capture interval, packet-loss report, and snap length.
  649: 
  650:     \item \textbf{Sensitive identifiers.} Stage~2 relations are currently formed from addresses retained in local metadata. Those addresses must be excluded from the thesis, shared CSVs, screenshots, and public metadata. Any released artefact requires stable pseudonymisation verified to preserve equality-based context membership.
  657: \chapter{Proposed Methodology}
  658: \label{sec:proposed_methodology}
  659: 
  660: Two complementary scales of traffic structure are represented by the detector. Stage~1 operates within a bidirectional flow, combining its ordered packets with statistical properties. Stage~2 relates the target flow to eligible historical flows. Sequential training and a fixed-dimensional Stage~1 interface keep intra-flow representation learning distinct from contextual reasoning across flows.
  661: 
  662: Although the architecture is derived from the Transformer \cite{Vaswani2017}, self-attention alone does not define the method. Three mechanisms are combined: (i) a cumulative-time coordinate that represents irregular packet arrival, (ii) an independently encoded statistical branch that modulates packet tokens within a bounded range, and (iii) causal target-query attention from the current flow to historical flows only. The distinction between packet order and elapsed time follows evidence from time-aware NIDS research \cite{Han2023GTID}. Separating within-flow evidence from cross-flow behaviour also addresses attacks whose signal is dispersed over several connections and may be missed when flows are classified independently
  663: \cite{LiuPatras2022NetSentry,Manocchio2024FlowTransformer, MartinReizabal2026FlowWindow}.
  664: 
  665: The high-level data path appears in Figure~\ref{fig:overall_framework}. A time-aware intra-flow Transformer converts packet records into one embedding for each flow. A sequence of these embeddings is then contextualised in Stage~2 to produce a binary decision for the target flow. Figure~\ref{fig:method_architecture} gives a more detailed view of statistical conditioning and target-query attention.
  666: 
  667: \begin{figure}[H]
  668:     \centering
  669:     \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{MScCS/overallFramework.png}
  670:     \caption{High-level architecture of the hierarchical detector. Stage~1 transforms a packet sequence into an intra-flow representation, and Stage~2 contextualises the resulting flow sequence before classifying the target flow.}
  671:     \label{fig:overall_framework}
  672: \end{figure}
  673: 
  674: \section{Architectural Overview}
  675: \label{subsec:method_architecture}
  676: 
  677: Flow $i$ is represented by a selected and padded packet-feature matrix
  678: $\mathbf{X}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d_p}$, a packet inter-arrival tensor
  679: $\mathbf{u}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$, a validity mask
  680: $\mathbf{m}_i\in\{0,1\}^{L}$, a statistical flow vector
  681: $\mathbf{s}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{d_f}$, and a binary target
  682: $y_i\in\{0,1\}$. The principal configuration uses $L=128$. Stage~1 computes
  683: 
  684: \begin{equation}
  685:     \left(\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(1)}_i,\mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i\right)
  686:     =F_{\theta_1}
  687:     \left(
  688:         \mathbf{X}_i,\mathbf{u}_i,\mathbf{m}_i,\mathbf{s}_i
  689:     \right),
  690:     \label{eq:method_stage1_mapping}
  691: \end{equation}
  692: 
  693: Here, $\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(1)}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{2}$ denotes the Stage~1 logits, and $\mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$ is the fused representation supplied to Stage~2. The principal model sets $d=128$.
  694: 
  695: Once all Stage~1 embeddings are available, flows are stably ordered by start time and flow identifier. Given relation $r$ and target $i$, the bounded causal input to Stage~2 is
  696: 
  697: \begin{equation}
  698:     \mathbf{Z}^{(r)}_i=
  699:     \left[
  700:         \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_{j_1},\ldots,
  701:         \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_{j_{K_i}},
  702:         \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i
  703:     \right],
  704:     \qquad j_k<i,\quad K_i\leq W-1,
  705:     \label{eq:method_stage2_input}
  706: \end{equation}
  707: 
  708: The current flow occupies the final valid position, and the principal setting uses $W=128$ tokens including that target. Stage~2 computes
  709: 
  710: \begin{equation}
  711:     \boldsymbol{\ell}^{(2,r)}_i
  712:     =G_{\theta_2}
  713:     \left(\mathbf{Z}^{(r)}_i,\boldsymbol{\mu}_i\right),
  714:     \label{eq:method_stage2_mapping}
  715: \end{equation}
  716: 
  717: where left padding is excluded by $\boldsymbol{\mu}_i$. Applying softmax to $\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(2,r)}_i$ yields the final class-1 probability.
  718: 
  719: As indicated in Figure~\ref{fig:method_architecture}, labels and Suricata alert fields serve only as targets and are absent from both encoders.
  720: 
  721: \begin{figure}[H]
  722:     \centering
  723:     \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{MScCS/ArchitecturalOverview.png}
  724:     \caption{Detailed architecture of the proposed two-stage detector. The statistical branch conditions Stage~1 packet tokens, while Stage~2 uses the target flow as the query and historical flows as keys and values.}
  725:     \label{fig:method_architecture}
  726: \end{figure}
