  728: \section{Stage~1: Intra-Flow Time-Aware Representation}
  729: \label{subsec:method_stage1}
  730: 
  731: A variable-length packet sequence is reduced by Stage~1 to one flow embedding. The principal processing path comprises record projection, time-aware sinusoidal encoding, flow-conditioned token modulation, pre-normalised Transformer layers, hybrid mask-aware pooling, and a two-class head.
  732: 
  733: \subsection{Packet-Record Projection}
  734: \label{subsubsec:packet_projection}
  735:

 287: \section{Alert-Derived Label Construction}
 288: \label{subsec:label_construction}
 289: 
 290: Binary targets are derived by the custom logger from Suricata's alert state. If $a(p_i)$ is the number of alerts associated with packet $p_i$, its packet-level target is
 291: \begin{equation}
 292:     y_i^{\mathrm{pkt}}
 293:     = \mathbb{I}\!\left[a(p_i)>0\right],
 294:     \label{eq:packet_alert_label}
 295: \end{equation}
 296: where $\mathbb{I}[\cdot]$ denotes the indicator function. A positive flow contains at least one packet that triggered an alert:
 297: \begin{equation}
 298:     y_f
 299:     = \max_{p_i\in\mathcal{P}_f} y_i^{\mathrm{pkt}},
 300:     \label{eq:flow_alert_label}
 301: \end{equation}
 302: Here, $\mathcal{P}_f$ contains all packets assigned to flow $f$. Accordingly, \datasetfield{packet_label}=1 identifies an alert-triggering packet, whereas \datasetfield{label}=1 marks an alert-associated flow.
 303: 
 304: These programmatically assigned targets are \emph{weak labels}, not ground truth verified independently by experts \cite{Ratner2017Snorkel}. Label~0 signifies only that the configured Suricata rules emitted no alert; benignity is not thereby established. Positive targets similarly inherit false alarms from the deployed ruleset. The reported experiments thus evaluate the learning and contextualisation of behaviour associated with Suricata alerts, not guaranteed coverage of every intrusion or zero-day attack. Errors in the labelling process may influence both model fitting and test estimates and must be considered when the findings are interpreted \cite{Northcutt2021ConfidentLearning}.
 305: 
 306: Direct target leakage is prevented by excluding \datasetfield{packet_label}, \datasetfield{label}, and all Suricata alert metadata from model inputs. Exact replication of the weak-labelling function would additionally depend on the Suricata version, a ruleset snapshot, the custom-module revision, its configuration, and the PCAP checksum. Where this provenance was not retained, the omission is reported in Chapter~\ref{sec:experimental_methodology} rather than filled retrospectively.
