 998: \section{Stage~2: Causal Inter-Flow Context Modelling}
 999: \label{subsec:method_stage2}
1000: 
1001: Stage~2 tests whether recent related behaviour changes the interpretation of a target flow. Its inputs are restricted to Stage~1 embeddings and context metadata; raw packet fields, aggregate flow features, and historical labels are not loaded.
1002: 
1003: \subsection{Embedding Interface and Chronological Order}
1004: \label{subsubsec:embedding_interface}
1005: 
1006: For each split, Stage~1 exports
1007: 
1008: \begin{equation}
1009:     \left(
1010:       \operatorname{flow\_id}_i,
1011:       y_i,
1012:       \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i,
1013:       t_i^{0},u_i,v_i,\rho_i
1014:     \right),
1015:     \label{eq:stage1_export_tuple}
1016: \end{equation}
1017: 
1018: where $t_i^{0}$ gives the start time, $u_i$ and $v_i$ identify source and destination, and $\rho_i$ records split membership. Stage~2 verifies identifier uniqueness, concatenates the split files, and applies a stable sort on $(t_i^{0},\operatorname{flow\_id}_i)$. Embeddings and metadata are reordered together to preserve alignment.
1019: 
1020: A deterministic loader without sampling is used for embedding export. The weighted training sampler draws with replacement and would otherwise repeat some flows while omitting others; it is therefore restricted to optimisation batches.
1021: 
1022: \subsection{Relation-Specific Causal Contexts}
1023: \label{subsubsec:context_relations}
1024: 
1025: Context construction proceeds once through the sorted flows in temporal order. Eligible earlier indices are retrieved before target $i$ is added to any history. Table~\ref{tab:context_relations} defines the four relations considered.
1026: 
1027: \begin{table}[H]
1028:     \centering
1029:     \caption{Stage~2 context relations.}
1030:     \label{tab:context_relations}
1031:     \small
1032:     \begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.0cm}p{4.0cm}p{7.1cm}@{}}
1033:         \toprule
1034:         Method & Eligibility condition for $j<i$ & Interpretation \\
1035:         \midrule
1036:         \methodfield{time_only} & Always eligible & Most recent flows globally, regardless of endpoint identity. \\
1037:         \methodfield{source_host} & $u_j=u_i$ & Earlier flows initiated by the same source identifier. \\
1038:         \methodfield{destination_host} & $v_j=v_i$ & Earlier flows directed to the same destination identifier. \\
1039:         \methodfield{endpoint} &
1040:         $\{u_j,v_j\}\cap\{u_i,v_i\}\neq\varnothing$ & Earlier flows sharing at least one endpoint in either role. \\
1041:         \bottomrule
1042:     \end{tabular}
1043: \end{table}
1044: 
1045: For a given relation $r$, define
1046: 
1047: \begin{equation}
1048:     \mathcal{A}^{(r)}_i
1049:     =\left\{j<i:R_r(i,j)=1\right\}.
1050:     \label{eq:method_eligible_context}
1051: \end{equation}
1052: 
1053: Split-policy filtering and deduplication are applied before retaining the latest $W-1$ indices:
1054: 
1055: \begin{equation}
1056:     \mathcal{C}^{(r)}_i
1057:     =\operatorname{Recent}_{W-1}
1058:       \left(\mathcal{A}^{(r)}_i\right).
1059:     \label{eq:recent_context}
1060: \end{equation}
1061: 
1062: Appending the current flow yields Equation~\ref{eq:method_stage2_input}. Histories are not updated until this sequence has been formed, preventing both self-inclusion and access to future items. Under the principal \methodfield{online} policy, a validation target may retrieve earlier training flows; a test target may retrieve earlier training, validation, and test flows. Future flows and historical labels remain unavailable. This policy approximates a detector whose state persists through chronological deployment.
1063: 
1064: In this implementation, ``causal'' refers to stable flow-start order: every context index occurs before its target index. The stronger condition $t_j^{\mathrm{end}}\leq t_i^{0}$ is not enforced during Stage~2 export. A long-lived overlapping flow can therefore precede the target even when its complete embedding would not have been available at the target's start time. What is evaluated is thus start-ordered, label-free context, rather than a fully timed streaming system. Production eligibility should instead be based on flow completion or embedding availability.
1065: 
1066: The \methodfield{time_only} relation denotes global chronological recency; it does not provide continuous inter-flow gaps to Stage~2. Explicit microsecond timing is confined to Stage~1, whereas Stage~2 represents relative age within the selected context.
1067: 
1068: Short sequences are padded on the left during collation, keeping the target at the final valid position:
1069: 
1070: \begin{equation}
1071:     [\mathrm{PAD},\ldots,\mathrm{PAD},
1072:       \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_{j_1},\ldots,
1073:       \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_{j_{K_i}},
1074:       \mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i].
1075:     \label{eq:left_padded_context}
1076: \end{equation}
1077: 
1078: A Boolean mask prevents the physical padding positions from affecting either positional encoding or attention.
1079: 
1080: \subsection{Context-Age Positional Encoding}
1081: \label{subsubsec:context_age_encoding}
1082: 
1083: For a target with $K_i$ historical tokens, age 0 is assigned to the target, age 1 to the most recent historical flow, and age $K_i$ to the oldest retained item. Writing this index as $a_{i,k}$, Stage~2 adds the sinusoidal encoding
1084: 
1085: \begin{align}
1195: 
1196: After Stage~1 has been trained for flow classification, its validation-selected checkpoint is restored. A deterministic loader without sampling exports one $\mathbf{z}^{(1)}_i$ and the required context metadata for every flow. These embeddings remain fixed during Stage~2 training, and no Stage~2 gradient reaches the earlier encoder. The sequential interface consequently allows Stage~1 comparisons to vary intra-flow representation while all Stage~2 models reason over a common representation.
