1256: \subsection{Stage~1 Baselines}
1257: \label{subsubsec:stage1_baselines}
1258: 
1259: Stage~1 is evaluated against the following baselines:
1260: 
1261: \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
1262:     \item \textbf{Flow-level MLP.} A multilayer perceptron consumes only the aggregated statistical flow vector. It provides the direct reference for testing whether packet-level sequence information adds value beyond conventional flow aggregation.
1263: 
1264:     \item \textbf{CNN1D.} Parallel one-dimensional convolutions with kernel sizes 3, 5, and 7 capture local packet motifs, followed by global pooling and classification. A second variant concatenates the log inter-arrival feature to each packet record. The design follows the general use of multi-kernel convolution for sequence classification \cite{Kim2014CNN}.
1265: 
1266:     \item \textbf{LSTM and BiLSTM.} Two-layer recurrent encoders with hidden width 128 and mean pooling provide unidirectional and bidirectional sequence references. The LSTM-with-time variant concatenates the stored log inter-arrival value to each packet vector
1267:     \cite{Hochreiter1997LSTM}.
1268: 
1269:     \item \textbf{GRU.} A two-layer, width-128 GRU with mean pooling tests whether a lighter gated recurrent encoder is sufficient \cite{Cho2014GRU}.
1270: 
1271: \end{itemize}
1272: 
1273: Packet tensors, masks, the chronological partition, focal objective, sampler, optimiser, training budget, and model-selection protocol are shared by all Stage~1 baselines. Parameter counts are not artificially matched. Instead, trainable parameters and runtime are reported beside predictive metrics to expose performance--efficiency trade-offs.
1274: 
1275: \subsection{Stage~1 Ablations and Sensitivity Tests}
1276: \label{subsubsec:stage1_ablation_protocol}
1277: 
1278: Four variants separate the effects of order and elapsed time: (i) joint positional and time-aware encoding, (ii) position only, (iii) time only, and (iv) neither coordinate. Packet features and Transformer capacity are unchanged. This control matters because appending an interval to a feature vector is not equivalent to using elapsed time as the coordinate of a sinusoidal sequence representation.
1279: 
1280: The three schemes in Table~\ref{tab:stage1_fusion_variants} assess how flow statistics should be incorporated. Scheme~A repeats the aggregate vector at every packet position; Scheme~B removes it; Scheme~C applies bounded token-FiLM conditioning from a separate branch. Packet-encoder and truncation experiments use Scheme~B to isolate sequence modelling. Scheme~C constitutes the complete Stage~1 model and provides embeddings for Stage~2.
1281: 
1282: Sequence-length sensitivity is tested at $L\in\{8,16,32,64,128,256\}$ with \texttt{head} selection. A separate experiment holds $L=128$ and contrasts \texttt{head}, \texttt{head\_tail}, and deterministic \texttt{random} sampling. No other setting changes in either sweep.
1283: 
1284: \subsection{Stage~2 Baselines and Context Ablations}
1285: \label{subsubsec:stage2_baseline_protocol}
1286: 
1287: Five controls are used to assess the target-query gated attention model:
1288: 
1289: \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
1290:     \item \textbf{No-context MLP} classifies only the current Stage~1 embedding and provides a minimal current-flow-only lower bound. Because it is not capacity matched to the contextual encoders, it is not by itself a causal estimate of the value of history.
1291: 
1292:     \item \textbf{Vanilla Transformer} applies full self-attention to the same context sequence and classifies its last valid token \cite{Vaswani2017}.
1293: 
1294:     \item \textbf{LSTM} and \textbf{GRU} replace attention with recurrent context encoders while preserving the Stage~1 inputs, context window, masks, and classifier protocol \cite{Hochreiter1997LSTM,Cho2014GRU}.
1295: 
1296:     \item \textbf{CNN+LSTM} applies parallel temporal convolutions with kernel sizes 3, 5, and 7 before a two-layer LSTM, combining local pattern extraction with recurrent context modelling \cite{Kim2014CNN,Hochreiter1997LSTM}.
1297: \end{itemize}
1298: 
1299: During architecture comparison, each contextual baseline receives the same \texttt{source\_host} histories at $W=128$, with the current flow occupying the final valid position. Since the target consumes one position, at most 127 historical flows are available. The no-context control keeps the target embedding and discards the preceding history.
1300: 
1301: Relation sensitivity holds the proposed encoder and $W=128$ fixed while varying eligibility among \texttt{time\_only}, \texttt{source\_host}, \texttt{destination\_host}, and \texttt{endpoint}. Window sensitivity instead fixes \texttt{source\_host} and evaluates $W\in\{16,32,64,128,256\}$. Run summaries contain predictive metrics and confusion matrices but do not retain target-level context occupancy or age. Interpretation of the window sweep therefore acknowledges that missing diagnostic information.
