1474: 
1475: Among these models, position+time gives the Transformer the best overall balance: macro-F1 reaches $0.8882$ and PR-AUC $0.8564$. LSTM+time is the strongest non-Transformer alternative by macro-F1, yet the Transformer improves this score by 2.05 percentage points. At the selected operating point, 95 additional positive flows are recovered at the cost of 10 more false positives. The gain therefore reflects a materially different error balance rather than a change in a summary score alone.
1476: 
1477: Encoding ablations separate two meanings of sequence location. Position records where a packet occurs, whereas cumulative time distinguishes a rapid burst from an identical order spread over a longer interval. Time-only encoding exceeds position-only, and combining them adds another 0.64 percentage points of macro-F1. Both FP and FN are also lower than under no encoding. These changes support order and elapsed time as complementary, rather than interchangeable, descriptions of a flow.
1478: 
1479: The lowest FPR, $0.8293\%$, belongs to the GRU, but that operating point misses 437 positive flows. Position+time introduces only 14 additional FP and removes 131 FN relative to the GRU. A low false-alert rate is therefore not sufficient by itself: conservative thresholds can suppress FP by transferring error to the positive class.
1480: 
1481: The flow-statistics MLP provides the direct aggregation-only control. It obtains macro-F1 $0.7762$ and PR-AUC $0.5604$ on the same test composition, compared with $0.8882$ and $0.8564$ for position+time. Its 853 FP and 501 FN fall to 296 and 306 when packet dynamics are modelled. Duration, count, and inter-arrival summaries therefore retain useful global information but fail to reconstruct all of the local order and timing present in the packet sequence.
1482: 
1483: Operating-point errors are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:results_stage1_errors}. Because the negative examples are shared, FP bar height is directly proportional to FPR.
